  "theory": {
        "intro": "The double angle identities give $\\sin 2A$, $\\cos 2A$ and $\\tan 2A$ from the ratios of $A$. Each comes from a sum identity with $B = A$. Cosine has three equivalent forms, so you can choose the one that suits the working.",
        "facts": [
"$\\sin 2A = 2\\sin A\\cos A$",
"$\\cos 2A = \\cos^{2}A - \\sin^{2}A$",
"$\\cos 2A = 2\\cos^{2}A - 1$, which uses $\\cos A$ only.",
"$\\cos 2A = 1 - 2\\sin^{2}A$, which uses $\\sin A$ only.",
"$\\tan 2A = \\dfrac{2\\tan A}{1 - \\tan^{2}A}$, provided $\\cos A \\ne 0$ and $1 - \\tan^{2}A \\ne 0$.",
"Rearranged to reduce powers: $\\cos^{2}A = \\dfrac{1 + \\cos 2A}{2}$ and $\\sin^{2}A = \\dfrac{1 - \\cos 2A}{2}$.",
"Read them backwards too: $2\\sin kx\\cos kx = \\sin 2kx$, $2\\cos^{2}kx - 1 = \\cos 2kx$ and $1 - 2\\sin^{2}kx = \\cos 2kx$."
],
        "steps": [
